  "narrative": "Special Note: Calves weaned over 45 days are left untagged Compared to last week (11/13/26). Feeder steers under 700 lbs sold generally steady on like quality offerings. Steer calves heavier than 700 lbs sold were too lightly tested to develop an accurate market trend, however lower undertones were noticed. Heifers were all too lightly tested to develop an accurate market trend however steady to higher undertones were noticed on 500-699 lbs heifers. Heifers under 500 lbs were not well compared. Quality this sale was mostly average with a few small consignments of attractive quality calves noticed. All offerings were in small packages this week with many singles noticed. Demand was mostly moderate, with moderate to good demand seen for very attractive offerings, especially for light to moderate weight calves. Heavy weight offerings, especially steers, were met with light demand as not enough heavy calves were seen for buyers to start loads. A small crowd of buyers was seen for todays sale, however internet bidding was active as several buyers needed to finish loads for the week. CME positions traded mostly sideways to end the week, posting very light losses Friday. Market activity was mostly moderate to active, despite a small buyer crowd. Weigh up cows sold on mostly light demand and offerings. Feeding cows and slaughter cows both were too lightly tested to develop any market trend. Slaughter bulls were too lightly tested last week to develop any market trend. Young age cows suitable to feed or rebreed were too lightly tested to develop any market trend. Supply included: 92% Feeder Cattle (57% Steers, 40% Heifers, 3% Bulls); 7% Slaughter Cattle (52% Cows, 48% Bulls); 0% Replacement Cattle (100% Stock Cows). Feeder cattle supply over 600 lbs was 52%.",
